# Supabase CLI local state — which project this checkout is linked to, plus
# cached remote version numbers. Per-machine, not per-repo: committing it would
# point a teammate's `db push` at whatever project the last person linked.
# The migrations themselves are tracked; only this scratch state is not.
supabase/.temp/

app-example

# generated native folders
/ios
/android

# Firebase service account keys — the FCM V1 credential.
#
# This is the file that actually grants authority to send push as this project,
# so it never enters the repo: it is uploaded to EAS once (`eas credentials`)
# and lives nowhere else. Globbed rather than named, because the filename
# carries a per-key hash and the next one Firebase issues will not match a
# literal entry. The previous line here was the exact filename *without* its
# `.json` extension, so it matched nothing and the key was sitting untracked in
# `git status`, one `git add .` from being published.
*-firebase-adminsdk-*.json
google-service-account*.json
service-account*.json

# NOT ignored, deliberately: `google-services.json`.
#
# It contains an `AIza…` Google API key, which is why secret scanners flag it,
# but Firebase documents these as non-secrets — the key identifies the project
# rather than authorising anything, and Security Rules plus App Check do the
# actual protecting. It has to reach the build, so it stays tracked. Restrict it
# to Firebase APIs in the Google Cloud console rather than hiding it.
